---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "six star protein powder",
      "six star whey protein powder",
      "six star 100% whey protein plus",
      "six star protein third-party tested",
      "six star protein walmart"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Cheap Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-cheap-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Six Star Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For Walmart shoppers eyeing the cheapest name-brand tub on the shelf: what is in Six Star 100% Whey Protein Plus, what the testing claim really covers, and what it costs per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#where-to-buy">Walmart, Amazon or Brand Site</a>
      <a href="#who-makes">Who Makes Six Star?</a>
      <a href="#buyers">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">Six Star vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Six Star protein powder is a cheap, ordinary whey blend: 30 g of protein in a 47 g scoop, about $1.34 per 25 g at the brand's own price, with sucralose and maltodextrin. It says it is banned-substance tested but names no certifier, so tested athletes should look elsewhere. Below we check the label, the testing claim, the price per gram, where to buy it and the alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Six Star Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Six Star 100% Whey Protein Plus is a decent budget whey and nothing more. It gives 30 g of protein per 47 g scoop, per the label at the time of writing, at about $1.34 per 25 g in the 1.7 lb tub and noticeably less in Walmart's 4.1 lb tub. The trade-offs are a scoop that is only about 64% protein, sucralose in every flavor, and a "banned-substance tested" claim with no certifier named.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you shop at Walmart, want 30 g of protein in one scoop for a low price, and do not mind artificial sweetener. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete, want a lean isolate with fewer carbs, or avoid sucralose. Those buyers have better options in our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> rankings.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Six Star by the Numbers (Label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 47 g scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">64%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein by weight</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.34</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g, 1.7 lb tub, brand price</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Named third-party certifiers</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Six Star and the Budget Wheys It Competes With
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first card is the Six Star tub Walmart stocks. Its genuine Amazon listing showed no live offer when we checked, so it links to a search. The other four are on Amazon with the price we saw on November 20, 2026, and the cost per 25 g of protein at that price.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Six Star Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Six Star says its products are banned-substance tested, but it does not name the lab or the certification program, and we could not find it in a public certifier registry. The brand's website carries an "Athlete Safe, Banned-Substance Tested" banner on every page, and a product image calls the formula a "Safe Choice banned substance free formula". None of that tells you who did the testing, how often, or which batches were covered.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters because the programs athletes rely on are public. NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport and Informed Choice each publish a searchable list of certified products, and the certified product carries the program's logo. When we searched the NSF Certified for Sport registry for Six Star on November 20, 2026, it returned no products. The Six Star product page we read shows no NSF or Informed logo on the tub images either. A brand-run or unnamed testing program may be perfectly sincere, but you cannot check it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most gym-goers this is a small point. Mainstream whey from a large manufacturer is rarely where banned substances turn up, and Six Star is made for Iovate Health Sciences, the same company behind MuscleTech, which has decades of mass-market supplement manufacturing behind it. If you are not subject to drug testing, an unnamed testing claim is not a reason on its own to avoid the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you compete in a tested sport, it is a reason. Athletes under NCAA, USADA or professional league rules should use a product listed by NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, and check the exact flavor in the registry before buying. At a similar price point, Muscle Milk Genuine is listed by NSF Certified for Sport, which is why it sits in the product cards above. Six Star also does not publish heavy-metal results that we could find, so if lead and cadmium are your main worry, our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">best protein powder without heavy metals</a> is the better starting point.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No registry listing, no certification.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">"Banned-substance tested" on a tub is a marketing claim until a named program lists the product. Search nsfsport.com or the Informed Sport database for the exact product and flavor before you rely on it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Six Star 100% Whey Protein Plus?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Six Star 100% Whey Protein Plus is a blend of whey protein concentrate, whey isolate and hydrolyzed whey, with maltodextrin as the second ingredient, per the label at the time of writing. The Triple Chocolate panel lists 30 g of protein, 180 calories, 8 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of total sugar with 0 g added, 3 g of fat and 220 mg of sodium in a 47 g scoop. Vanilla Cream is 170 calories in the same 47 g scoop, and Strawberry Smoothie is 170 calories in 46 g.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
