                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose and stevia (Reb A)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The number to notice is protein density. Thirty grams of protein from a 47 g scoop is about 64% protein by weight. A typical isolate-led whey such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard gives 24 g from about 31 g, roughly 77%. The difference is mostly maltodextrin, a cheap carbohydrate that adds bulk and body, plus cocoa, flavors and minerals. You still get the protein you paid for, but you also get around 30 to 40 extra calories per serving compared with a leaner whey dosed to the same 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the ingredient list is standard for a budget whey: natural and artificial flavors, soy and/or sunflower lecithin for mixing, calcium carbonate, salt, a gum blend of guar gum, gum acacia and xanthan gum, sucralose, and ascorbic acid and zinc oxide for the immune-support claim. Each scoop provides 45 mg of vitamin C and 5.5 mg of zinc, half the daily value of each, and about 250 mg of calcium in the chocolate. Six Star also claims 6.6 g of naturally occurring BCAAs per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two label details are easy to miss. Each scoop lists 85 mg of cholesterol, 28% of the daily value, which is normal for a whey concentrate blend but worth knowing if your doctor has you watching it. And the strawberry flavor adds beet juice for color and rebaudioside A, a stevia extract, alongside sucralose. All three flavors declare milk and soy, and the tubs are made in a facility that also handles wheat, egg, tree nuts, peanuts, sesame, fish and shellfish, so people with serious allergies should read the tub in hand.
          </p>
        </div>

