        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where Can You Buy Six Star: Walmart, Amazon or the Brand Site?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Walmart is the easiest and usually the cheapest place to buy Six Star 100% Whey Protein Plus, because it stocks both the 1.8 lb and the 4.1 lb tubs in store and online. Walmart's listings include Triple Chocolate, Vanilla Cream, Strawberry Smoothie, Cookies and Cream and Kellogg's Froot Loops versions, and Six Star is one of the anchors of the protein aisle in our <a href="/best-walmart-protein-powder/">best Walmart protein powder</a> guide. Prices move with Rollbacks, so check the unit price on the shelf tag.
          </p>

          <p>
            Amazon is a weaker option for this particular tub. The genuine 1.82 lb Triple Chocolate listing showed no live offer when we checked, and the other 100% Whey Protein Plus listing we found was sold only by a third-party reseller at $99.97 for 2 lb, more than three times the brand's price. Amazon itself was selling Six Star Complete Whey Protein Plus, a different formula. If you do buy on Amazon, read our guide to <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">buying protein powder on Amazon</a> for how to spot a reseller listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Six Star's own website sells the 1.7 lb tub in three flavors for $28.99, with free shipping on orders over $30 and a Subscribe and Save discount, per the site at the time of writing. The site's terms say it is owned by Iovate and that orders are fulfilled by iHerb, so you are buying from an authorized source. The catch is that the site only listed the small tub, which is the worst value per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grocery chains are the other channel. Kroger, Safeway and Albertsons banners, Rite Aid and Vitacost have all listed Six Star whey, often in the small tub at a higher price than Walmart. Pharmacies are rarely the cheap place for protein, and our sibling guide to <a href="/cvs-protein-powder/">CVS protein powder</a> walks through what drugstore shelves carry and what they charge. For the lowest price, the Walmart 4.1 lb tub is the one to look for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Who Makes Six Star, and Is It the Same as MuscleTech?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Six Star is owned by Iovate Health Sciences International, the Canadian company that also owns MuscleTech, per the terms of use on Six Star's website. The two brands share a parent, but they are aimed at different shelves. MuscleTech is the supplement-store and Amazon brand with more specialized formulas, while Six Star is priced and packaged for mass retailers such as Walmart, and marketed to athletes, with listings pitching it for game day as much as the gym.
          </p>

          <p>
            The family resemblance shows on the label. Six Star 100% Whey Protein Plus and MuscleTech Nitro-Tech both deliver 30 g of protein in a scoop of roughly 46 to 47 g, both use a concentrate, isolate and hydrolyzed whey blend, and both rely on sucralose. The differences are the extras: Nitro-Tech adds 3 g of creatine monohydrate and free amino acids, while Six Star adds vitamin C and zinc and keeps the formula simpler. Neither names a third-party certifier on the pages we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Six Star's marketing calls it America's number one selling sports nutrition brand, a claim printed on product images on its website. We cannot verify sales rankings, and they say nothing about quality anyway. What the size of the brand does tell you is that Six Star is easy to find, has a long track record, and is unlikely to disappear from the shelf next month, which matters if you want to reorder the same tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you like the Iovate approach but want more choice, MuscleTech's range is broader, with a grass-fed whey without artificial sweeteners and a cheaper Ripped version at times. If you want creatine in the same scoop, compare our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-creatine/">best protein powder with creatine</a> guide. Six Star itself also sells separate creatine and pre-workout products, though they are outside the scope of this review.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Six Star Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly rate Six Star well, and taste and mixing drive both the praise and the complaints. We have not tasted it ourselves. A third-party analysis of reviews on Walmart's Triple Chocolate 1.81 lb listing counted about 388 reviews in the past 12 months with an average of about 4.1 stars, and found that flavor and mixability were the most common subject of five-star reviews and of negative ones alike.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the positive side, review summaries we found say the chocolate flavors get the most praise, that the powder mixes easily in a shaker with water or milk, and that it is good value for a 30 g scoop. Positive reviews tend to treat it as a dependable everyday shake rather than a dessert, and value for money comes up again and again.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common complaints are a bland or watery taste, especially with water, an artificial sweetness some people dislike in the vanilla, occasional clumping, and price swings between visits. A few reviewers mention bloating, which is plausible with a concentrate-based whey that contains lactose; if dairy bothers you, a lactose-free option from our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">best lactose-free protein powder</a> list will likely sit better. Some negative reviews are about delivery and damaged tubs rather than the powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaways are simple. Start with a chocolate flavor in the small tub before committing to 4.1 lb, mix it with milk if you find it thin, and use a shaker with a mixing ball or a blender. If you want a richer, creamier shake, Muscle Milk Genuine's added fat does that at the cost of calories, while Six Star's Kellogg's cereal-flavor tubs are an option if the standard flavors feel too plain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Six Star vs Body Fortress, Muscle Milk and Equate: Which Budget Whey Is Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Six Star and Body Fortress are nearly interchangeable, Muscle Milk Genuine is the one with a sport certification, and Equate is the leaner, cheaper store-brand scoop. All four sit in the same Walmart aisle, so the right choice depends on what you care about after price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Six Star vs Body Fortress</h3>

          <p>
            Both give 30 g of protein in a 45 to 47 g scoop at about 170 to 180 calories, both add vitamins and minerals for an immune-support claim, and both cost about the same in matching tub sizes. Body Fortress has one advantage: it carries an NSF Contents Certified claim, which checks that the label is accurate, although it does not screen for banned substances. Buy whichever is cheaper per 25 g that week, and see our <a href="/body-fortress-protein-powder-review/">Body Fortress protein powder review</a> for its full label.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Six Star vs Muscle Milk Genuine</h3>

          <p>
            Muscle Milk Genuine gives 32 g of protein per two-scoop, 270-calorie serving and is listed by NSF Certified for Sport, so it is the better budget pick for tested athletes. It is a heavier shake, with 9 g of fat from added oils, and the tub runs out after 13 servings. Six Star is the leaner of the two and better if you just want protein. Our <a href="/muscle-milk-protein-powder-review/">Muscle Milk protein powder review</a> covers its other formulas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Six Star vs Equate and Gold Standard</h3>

          <p>
            Walmart's Equate whey and Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard both give 24 g of protein from roughly 31 g of powder, so they are about 77% protein against Six Star's 64%. If you want fewer carbs and calories per gram of protein, either is the better formula, and Equate is often cheaper per 25 g as well. Our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-vs-body-fortress/">Optimum Nutrition vs Body Fortress</a> comparison covers the same lean-versus-big-scoop trade-off, and the <a href="/protein-powder-comparison/">compare tool</a> lines up label data for the products in our database.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Six Star Protein Powder, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Six Star is a good fit for budget-minded lifters, busy adults and teenagers who want an easy 30 g of protein from a tub they can grab at Walmart. If you drink one shake a day after training, mix it with milk, and buy the 4.1 lb tub when the price is right, it does that job for less than most name-brand powders. It also suits people who simply want one big scoop rather than two smaller ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is a weaker fit for three groups. Drug-tested athletes should choose a powder listed by NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport instead, because Six Star's testing claim cannot be checked. People avoiding artificial sweeteners will not find a sucralose-free Six Star flavor; our list of the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">best protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> has alternatives. And anyone counting carbs or calories closely will get more protein per gram of powder from an isolate, as covered in our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are also health cases where a doctor or registered dietitian should have the final say. People with kidney disease, those who are pregnant or breastfeeding, older adults with medical conditions, and anyone on medication that interacts with high protein intake should ask before adding a 30 g shake to their day. Six Star contains milk and soy, so it is not suitable for anyone allergic to either, and the 85 mg of cholesterol per scoop is worth mentioning to your doctor if you are managing cholesterol.
          </p>

          <p>
            For teenage athletes, 30 g of protein in one shake is generous; many teens can meet their needs from food, with a shake as a convenience rather than a necessity. If a high school or college athlete is subject to drug testing, the certification point above applies to them too, and a certified product is the safer choice. Whatever you buy, compare cost per 25 g on the day rather than the sticker price.
          </p>
        </div>

