            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating "banned-substance tested" as a certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Six Star's banner says athlete safe and banned-substance tested, but it names no program and the product was not in the NSF Certified for Sport registry when we searched. If you are drug-tested, that difference can matter. Only a product listed in a public registry, for your exact flavor, counts as certified.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the small tub out of habit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 1.7 to 1.8 lb tub is the most expensive way to buy Six Star per gram. The 4.1 lb Walmart tub holds roughly 40 scoops, more than twice as many, and usually costs well under twice as much. Once you know you like the flavor, the big tub is the one to buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pricing the wrong Six Star product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Six Star Complete Whey Protein Plus on Amazon is a newer sustained-release formula, not the 100% Whey Protein Plus sold at Walmart. At $44.99 for about 20 scoops when we checked, it costs far more per 25 g. Read the product name on the listing before comparing prices.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the carbohydrate in the scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 47 g scoop with 30 g of protein still has about 8 g of carbohydrate, mostly maltodextrin, and 170 to 180 calories. That is fine for most people after training, but if you are cutting hard or counting carbs, a 24 g isolate scoop with almost no carbs fits a tight budget better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying reseller prices on Amazon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When we checked, one 2 lb Six Star 100% Whey Protein Plus listing on Amazon was sold only by a third-party seller at $99.97, more than three times the brand's price. Check who the seller is, compare with the brand site and Walmart, and walk away from a listing priced like that.
              </p>
            </div>
